00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:53.880
Canadian investors face a structural challenge when US indices concentrate risk in a handful of mega-cap names, while the TSX spreads exposure more broadly, but with less upside capture from those same growth leaders.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:02.880
Rosenberg highlights how pairing a core TSX sleeve with selective US or international exposure can offset single stock concentration.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:16.820
The approach calls for a 60% allocation to a broad TSX ETF, such as XIU, and 40% to a US small cap or international ETF, such as VIOO or VXUS.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.500
Quarterly rebalancing keeps any sleeve from drifting more than 5 percentage points from target.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:31.500
Historical results from the 2022 rate hike cycle showed roughly 15% smaller drawdowns compared with pure S&amp;P 500 exposure.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.580
The same mix also picked up incremental Canadian dividend yield that the S&amp;P 500 tends to underweight.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.260
Correlation spikes during global shocks remain the main risk that can shrink the intended buffer.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:51.800
US investors gain the opposite benefit by adding international or small cap sleeves to dilute mega-cap dominance.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.540
Low-cost ETFs make the rebalancing practical inside a TFSA without triggering frequent trading concerns.

