00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.100
Now here is something most retail investors get wrong when they first open a chart.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:48.060
Imagine you bought a junior minor last week after positive drill results pushed it above its 50 day moving average.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:55.860
Your order filled at the open but the real mechanics involve checking whether price respected that average as support on the daily chart.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.700
And whether RSI 14 stayed below 70 to avoid chasing an overbought move.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:08.340
Volume above the 20 day average on the breakout day adds confirmation that institutions are participating.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.360
MACDE crossovers can flag momentum shifts earlier than simple price action.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:22.100
Professionals always compare the current setup against the 200 day average to distinguish short-term noise from longer-term trends.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:30.080
What most retail investors do not realize is that technical tools excel at timing entries and exits within an existing trend

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.840
but fail when used to predict major reversals without fundamental catalysts.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:41.500
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every moving average bounce as a high probability trade.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:48.960
Instead require at least one confirming indicator such as rising volume or RSI divergence before committing capital.

